+@node Apple Blocks
+@subsection Apple Blocks
+@cindex Blocks
+@cindex block pointer
+@cindex @code{__block}
+
+With @option{-fblocks}, GCC accepts Apple Blocks syntax in C and
+Objective-C.  A block pointer uses @code{^} in place of @code{*}:
+
+@smallexample
+typedef int (^operation_t) (int);
+
+int apply (operation_t operation, int value)
+@{
+  return operation (value);
+@}
+@end smallexample
+
+A block literal contains an optional parameter list followed by a compound
+statement.  Its result type is inferred from its return statements, or may be
+written explicitly:
+
+@smallexample
+operation_t add (int amount)
+@{
+  return ^(int value) @{ return value + amount; @};
+@}
+
+operation_t twice (void)
+@{
+  return ^int(int value) @{ return value * 2; @};
+@}
+@end smallexample
+
+Automatic variables are captured by value and are read-only in the block.
+The @code{__block} storage qualifier makes a local variable mutable and
+shared by the enclosing function and its blocks:
+
+@smallexample
+__block int count = 0;
+void (^increment) (void) = ^ @{ ++count; @};
+@end smallexample
+
+Generated code uses the Apple Blocks ABI, including global and stack block
+classes, descriptors, structure-return flags, byref forwarding cells, and
+copy/dispose helpers for byref variables, block pointers, and Objective-C
+object pointers.  The runtime that provides the block classes and the
+copy and release entry points is selected with
+@option{-fblocks-runtime=}.  On Darwin, Mac OS X 10.6 and later provide
+a system Blocks runtime; for older deployment targets GCC uses the
+PLBlocks compatibility runtime, @samp{libPLBlocks}, and links it
+automatically.
+
+The preprocessor macro @code{__BLOCKS__} is defined to @code{1}, and
